<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huron Carol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">probably <a href="/years/1642">1642</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Jesous Ahatonhia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The &quot;Huron Carol&quot; is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> hymn, counted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s oldest Christmas song. It was written, probably in 1642, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-de-brebeuf">Jean de Brébeuf</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sainte-marie-among-the-hurons">Sainte-Marie among the Hurons</a>, in the language of the Huron-Wendat people. Its Wendat title is &quot;Jesous Ahatonhia&quot;, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, he is born&quot;. The tune is that of a traditional French folk song, &quot;Une Jeune Pucelle&quot; (&quot;A Young Maid&quot;).</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The &quot;<b>Huron Carol</b>&quot; is a Christmas hymn, counted as Canada's oldest Christmas song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/huron-carol">Huron Carol</a></li></ul></section>
